{"topic": "science", "body": "Cities are consistently warmer than the countryside around them, a phenomenon researchers call the urban heat island effect. The cause is largely structural: asphalt and concrete absorb solar radiation during the day and release it slowly after dark, while the vertical surfaces of tall buildings trap heat that would otherwise escape upward. Vegetation, which cools the air through evaporation, is scarce in dense districts. The result is a temperature gap that can reach five degrees Celsius on a still summer night. For much of the twentieth century this was treated as a curiosity rather than a problem. That changed as heat waves grew more frequent and their death tolls became harder to ignore. Studies of the 2003 European heat wave found that urban residents died at markedly higher rates than rural ones, and that the difference persisted even after accounting for age and income. Municipal responses have tended toward the visible: planting trees, painting roofs white, adding parks. These measures work, but their effect is local and modest. A white roof cools the building beneath it far more than the street outside. Some researchers argue that meaningful change requires rethinking how cities are zoned and built, a far slower and more contentious undertaking than planting a row of trees.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is mainly concerned with", "correct_answer": "why cities retain heat and why the common responses to it achieve less than they appear to", "distractors": ["the medical consequences of the 2003 European heat wave", "the history of zoning law in European cities", "techniques for measuring temperature differences between cities and countryside"], "explanation": "The passage first explains the structural causes of urban heat, then argues that visible municipal measures are modest in effect. The heat wave and zoning are mentioned in service of that argument rather than as the subject, and no measurement technique is described anywhere.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, vegetation cools the surrounding air by", "correct_answer": "evaporation", "distractors": ["reflecting solar radiation back upward", "blocking wind at street level", "absorbing carbon from the atmosphere"], "explanation": "The passage states directly that vegetation cools the air through evaporation. Reflection is what a white roof does, and neither wind nor carbon absorption is mentioned in connection with cooling.", "difficulty_est": 2}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The passage suggests that the 2003 heat wave findings were significant because they", "correct_answer": "showed the temperature gap had consequences that could not be explained by who happened to live where", "distractors": ["were the first measurements of urban temperatures ever taken", "demonstrated that white roofs reduce mortality", "led Parliament to change how cities are zoned"], "explanation": "The detail that the difference persisted after accounting for age and income is what rules out the alternative explanation that city populations simply differ from rural ones. The passage never claims the study was a first, never links roofs to mortality, and describes zoning reform as something researchers argue for rather than something achieved.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the last sentence, \"contentious\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "likely to provoke disagreement", "distractors": ["expensive to carry out", "technically complex", "poorly understood"], "explanation": "The word describes rethinking how cities are zoned and built, set against the ease of planting trees — the contrast is about political friction, not cost or difficulty. The sentence already supplies \"slower\" for the practical objection, so the second word must carry something else.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author mentions painting roofs white primarily in order to", "correct_answer": "give an example of a measure whose benefits are narrower than they first appear", "distractors": ["recommend it as the most effective municipal response available", "contrast its success with the failure of tree planting", "explain the physical process by which solar radiation is reflected"], "explanation": "The white roof appears in a paragraph arguing that visible measures are local and modest, and the following sentence limits its benefit to the building beneath it. The author groups it with tree planting rather than against it, and never explains the physics of reflection.", "difficulty_est": 3}]}
{"topic": "history", "body": "Before the railways, time in Britain was a local matter. Each town set its clocks by the sun, so noon in Bristol fell roughly ten minutes after noon in London. For most purposes the discrepancy was invisible; a farmer had no reason to care what hour it was two hundred miles away. Railway timetables made the difference impossible to ignore. A train leaving London at nine and arriving in Bristol at eleven was, by Bristol's clocks, arriving at ten fifty. Companies began publishing schedules in London time and instructing stations to adopt it, a practice that spread along the lines themselves. By 1855 most public clocks in Britain showed a single standard time, though the change was not universally welcomed: some towns kept two minute hands on their clock faces, one for railway time and one for their own. The legal recognition of standard time came later, and almost accidentally. In 1858 a court in Dorchester dismissed a case because the defendant had arrived at the courthouse at the correct local time but after the hour set by railway time. The resulting confusion prompted Parliament to act, though it took until 1880 for a statute to define what time it legally was.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The passage is primarily concerned with", "correct_answer": "how a commercial necessity produced a national standard that the law only later confirmed", "distractors": ["the engineering difficulties of building railway lines across Britain", "a legal dispute over the scheduling of court appearances", "the reasons Bristol and London developed at different rates"], "explanation": "The passage traces standard time from a railway convenience to an 1880 statute, with the law arriving last. The Dorchester case is one step in that sequence rather than the subject, and neither engineering nor regional development is discussed.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the passage, by 1855", "correct_answer": "most public clocks in Britain displayed a single standard time", "distractors": ["Parliament had defined standard time by statute", "railway companies had abandoned local time entirely", "every town in Britain had adopted two minute hands"], "explanation": "The passage gives 1855 as the point when most public clocks showed one standard time. The statute came in 1880, and the two minute hands were something only some towns did.", "difficulty_est": 2}, {"kind": "inference", "prompt": "The detail about clock faces with two minute hands suggests that some towns", "correct_answer": "accepted the practical need for railway time without giving up their own", "distractors": ["found railway time to be frequently inaccurate", "were required by Parliament to display both times", "profited from selling modified clocks to travellers"], "explanation": "Keeping both hands shows a town using railway time while preserving local time, which is what the passage means by the change not being universally welcomed. Parliament had not yet acted at that point, and nothing suggests railway time was unreliable or commercially exploited.", "difficulty_est": 4}, {"kind": "word_in_context", "prompt": "As used in the third sentence, \"discrepancy\" most nearly means", "correct_answer": "difference", "distractors": ["delay", "complaint", "measurement"], "explanation": "The word refers back to the ten-minute gap between noon in Bristol and noon in London, which is a difference rather than a delay — neither town was late. No complaint or act of measuring is described.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "authors_purpose", "prompt": "The author describes the 1858 Dorchester case primarily to", "correct_answer": "show that legal recognition trailed a change that had already taken hold in practice", "distractors": ["criticize the court for dismissing the case", "illustrate how unreliable railway schedules had become", "explain the specific wording Parliament adopted in 1880"], "explanation": "The case is introduced immediately after the claim that legal recognition came later and almost accidentally, and it demonstrates law catching up to established practice. The author passes no judgement on the court and never quotes the statute.", "difficulty_est": 4}]}
